Ladybird’s seccomp policy allows pwrite64() and answers pwritev2()
with ENOSYS, so musl’s pwrite() falls back to pwrite64(). Gecko’s
SandboxFilter.cpp allows pwrite64() and has no rule for pwritev2(),
which then gets InvalidSyscall(): a trap whose BlockedSyscallTrap()
also answers ENOSYS. Chromium’s syscall_sets.cc allows pwrite64()
and lists neither pwritev() nor pwritev2(). From reading the sources.
